== D9Class {"type": "string", "enum": ["success", "policy-failed", "request-rejected", "indeterminate", "operational-failed", "interrupted"]}
== D9FaultCause {"type": "string", "enum": ["none", "host-io", "ledger-busy", "ledger-corrupt", "cas-link", "provider-protocol", "durability-commit", "delivery-required", "output-serialization", "extension-install-io", "serve-protocol", "host-invariant"], "description": "Which operational fault applies. `host-invariant` is the successor member and its scope is narrow: the closed D9ErrorCode vocabulary already published SYSTEM.OUTCOME.ILLEGAL_STATE with no cause mapping to it, so the host-INVARIANT subtype specifically had no representable operational-failed termination, because that class requires BOTH an errorCode and a non-none faultCause. Other host-fault causes - host-io, ledger-corrupt, provider-protocol and the rest - already existed and were always representable. PRECISION: codeMaps.rule's `total and injective` is a property of the DECLARED CAUSE DOMAIN, so an errorCode without a preimage was never a violation of totality, and this member does not `make the maps total`. It adds no error code, class, exit code or reason code, but it IS a vocabulary extension: faultCause grows by one. The inherited docs/coop/artifacts/d9-exit-contract.v1.14.json is HISTORICAL and unchanged, so its own enum still omits the member; the selected composition is native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry/hostInvariantSuccessor and publishing the successor D9 artifact belongs to that unit. Borrowing host-io for a host invariant violation is refused by contract: an I/O failure and an invariant violation are different remedies."}
